Step 9

Step 1:
AI was not involved in this step; it was set up manually.

Step 2:
For the initial setup, we needed to get a new extension called GitLens to allow Copilot 
to create the issue on GitHub. We also had to get a skill called prompt engineering which 
was designed with the intention of refining prompts and making them more effective in mind. 
The first iteration, the original issue, didn’t follow course notes, so we added the entire 
course notes as context for the following iteration and received a way better response out 
of it.

Step 3 
We just asked it to review and make sure code doesn’t conflict or duplicate existing work 
and after a thorough search it said everything was correct. After another review, the agent 
confirmed the correction and we moved onwards

Step 4: 
For this step basically asked copilot to check our files and the issue then to restate it 
back to me before any code changes were made. After reviewing the changes Copilot were 
making everything look solid, but we made sure to compare it to the requirements as 
to not deviate. No remarkable changes were made this step, as we mostly ensured quality 
of the work. we then moved o to step 5.

Step 5:
Here, the agent created an adequate implementation plan by following the set instructions 
without fault. However, the plan it produced was missing extra information necessary for 
it to confirm web 3 milestone 2’s standard. Also, the tests should be rewritten, since it’s 
incomplete and requires more depth to be excellent. The biggest issue relates to remark on 
the next step: it based itself on a file structure that does not follow what we did in class. 
Otherwise, the plan is overly detailed and meets the requirements.

Step 6:
The agent cooperated seamlessly according to the implementation plan. However, during this 
step it was acknowledged that the plan should be updated so that the file structure could 
closely resemble the file structure we made in class and in our labs. As such, the bot 
technically did wrong by executing exactly what was asked. When the agent executed the new
plan, it went as expected.

Step 7:
The solution was validated with the current tests used as a benchmark for what is acceptable. 
Then, it added the subsequent update tests as requested without a big hiccup. After a final 
review, it was confirmed that the AI labored correctly. things vere further tweaked on just 
to refine its quality before we movedon to the next step.

Step 8:
For this step, we had Copilot come up with a pr title and description. These were simple and
straightforward. After adding it as a reviewer, the beginnings were rocky, since the quota 
limit was reached. Eventually, when it wanted to cooperate, it gave a detailed review, 
specifying change of importance and level of effort used. For each discrepancy found, a 
conversation was initiated to allow it to fix the issue by adding it to secrets, for example. 
These conversations usually exceed one prompt, meaning that the copilot was functioning 
smoothly.



